WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.809
Binary protocol security starts with a disciplined boundary mindset.

2
00:00:03.809 --> 00:00:11.850
Treat every byte as untrusted until framing, length checks, field parsing, state rules, and required trust checks have succeeded.

3
00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:18.198
Define message boundaries and limits before parsing deeply, and separate incomplete data from invalid data.

4
00:00:18.198 --> 00:00:19.467
Make parsing explicit.

5
00:00:19.467 --> 00:00:22.430
Check remaining bytes before every field read.

6
00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:25.392
Convert raw bytes into typed values deliberately.

7
00:00:25.392 --> 00:00:33.433
Validate endianness, signedness, numeric ranges, offsets, counts, and derived size calculations before using them for memory, indexing, state, or

8
00:00:33.433 --> 00:00:33.856
policy.

9
00:00:33.856 --> 00:00:36.395
Keep protocol state and compatibility visible.

10
00:00:36.395 --> 00:00:42.320
Accept only supported versions and features, reject unsupported behavior safely, and test state transitions.

11
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:51.207
When authentication, integrity, or freshness matters, use approved mechanisms and act only after the message is trusted for the current context.

12
00:00:51.207 --> 00:00:53.746
Finally, bound resources and validate continuously.

13
00:00:53.746 --> 00:00:57.978
Limit memory, time, nesting, decompression, rates, and retries where relevant.

14
00:00:57.978 --> 00:01:06.442
Test malformed and boundary cases, use fuzzing defensively, monitor safe error categories, and review binary protocol parsers as security-critical

15
00:01:06.442 --> 00:01:07.289
C++ code.

16
00:01:07.289 --> 00:01:16.599
Small parsing assumptions can influence availability, memory safety, and trust decisions, so the habits should be part of normal design and code

17
00:01:16.599 --> 00:01:19.562
review for every release and maintenance cycle.

18
00:01:19.562 --> 00:01:24.640
That discipline keeps compact formats manageable and trustworthy as ownership changes later.
